## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical memory management, lock-free data structures, and safe object reuse under contention. A beginner-friendly allocator emphasizes alignment and simple reclaim strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free freelist using CAS
- ABA avoidance (tagged pointers or hazard pointers)
- Slab-style pool with 64-byte blocks
- Cache-line awareness and fragmentation

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-code for allocate/free
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; } Block;
typedef struct Pool { Block* head; void* pool; size_t block_size; } Pool;

Block* allocate(Pool* p){
  Block* old = atomic_load(&p->head);
  while (old){
    Block* next = old->next;
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->head, &old, next)){
      return old;
    }
  }
  return NULL;
}
void deallocate(Pool* p, Block* b){
  Block* head = atomic_load(&p->head);
  do {
    b->next = head;
  } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->head, &head, b));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect pool exhaustion and recover?
- How would you extend to variable-size objects or per-object headers?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical low-level memory management in NUMA systems, emphasizing locality, contention, and false sharing—key for latency-sensitive trading/analytics workloads.

## Key Concepts

- NUMA locality and per-node free lists
- Lock-free/atomic free-list operations and ABA avoidance
- Cross-node migration policies and migration costs
- Cache-line alignment and false sharing avoidance
- Validation: microbenchmarks with mixed-local/global allocations, perf measurements

## Code Example

```cpp
// simplified per-node free-list CAS skeleton
#include <atomic>

struct Node {
  std::atomic<void*> head{nullptr};
};

void* alloc(Node& node) {
  void* h = node.head.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
  if (h) {
    void* next = *(void**)h;
    if (node.head.compare_exchange_weak(h, next, std::memory_order_acquire, std::memory_order_relaxed))
      return h;
  }
  // fallback: steal from other node or central pool
  return nullptr;
}
```

